;; ⭐ Stone 255.12, site 2 — the ACTUAL shape of `wat/holon/Ngram.wat`'s
;; conversion failure, and the row that says why 255.12 REPORTED it instead of
;; curing it.
;;
;; Two registrations of one macro, identical but for the dialect — AND the
;; quasiquoted TEMPLATE carries a `fn` whose annotation markers the codemod
;; rewrites `<-` → `:-` and `->` → `:-`. Those are SYMBOL `<-` / `->` against
;; KEYWORD `:-`: three different tokens, not three spellings of one name.
;; `canonical_identity("<-")` is `"<-"` and `canonical_identity(":-")` is `":-"`,
;; so NO identity door equates them and `is_reference()` never gets a say.
;;
;; Curing this would mean teaching a blind structural walker that the retired
;; arrow grammar and the current one are interchangeable INSIDE A TEMPLATE —
;; and, because the codemod folds BOTH arrows onto one marker, that `<-` ≡ `->`.
;; Two templates that emit different surface forms would become one macro. That
;; is a grammar-migration question (8d-ii converts the baked sources too), not an
;; identity question, and it is the builder's call.
(:wat::core::defmacro :p255_12::MA [x <- :wat::WatAST] -> :wat::WatAST
  `(:wat::core::fn [y <- :wat::core::i64] -> :wat::core::i64 (:wat::i64::+ y ~x)))
(:wat::core::defmacro :p255_12::MA [x <- wat/WatAST] :- wat/WatAST
  `(wat.core/fn [y :- wat.core/i64] :- wat.core/i64 (wat.i64/+ y ~x)))
